\chapter{Berkeley Makes a Larger Unix}
\label{ch:bsd}
\index{BSD}\index{Berkeley Software Distribution}\index{Joy, Bill}\index{VAX}

\section{A distribution before a separate operating system}

The Berkeley Software Distribution began as additions to an existing Unix
environment. Its name describes an activity: collecting useful software
and sending it to other sites. Early distributions were not unrestricted,
self-contained replacements for AT\&T's system. Recipients generally
needed an appropriate Unix license. A later BSD identity grew out of this
practice rather than appearing fully formed with the first tape.

Universities supplied a distinctive development setting. They had students
who could spend intensive periods improving tools, researchers who needed
new facilities, and links to other institutions with similar problems.
They also had turnover. A successful improvement needed documentation and
interfaces that outlived its immediate author. Sharing a distribution made
a local change available for review and reuse elsewhere.

Bill Joy's work on editors and the C shell is part of this story, but BSD
was never the work of one programmer alone. Bob Fabry, Chuck Haley, Özalp
Babaoglu, Marshall Kirk McKusick, Sam Leffler, Mike Karels, Keith Bostic,
and many others contributed at different periods. The Computer Systems
Research Group became a center where contributions could be integrated
into a coherent system. Integration was a form of engineering, not merely
administration \parencite[ch. 8]{kernighan-history}.

\section{The VAX changes the scale of the problem}

The PDP-11 was an excellent home for a compact system, but its address-space
limits constrained large programs. DEC's VAX offered a 32-bit architecture
and hardware support for virtual memory. A port could make Unix run on the
new machine without immediately exploiting all those capabilities.
That was the situation with Unix/32V, derived from Seventh Edition through
work by John Reiser and Tom London at Bell Labs.

Berkeley's virtual-memory work changed the practical possibilities.
A process could address more memory than it had resident at a particular
moment. The kernel and hardware cooperated to bring pages into physical
memory as required. Larger symbolic programs, scientific applications,
and development tools became more feasible. The programming model still
looked recognizably Unix even though memory management had become much
more elaborate.

This is a recurring pattern in the history: retain a useful external
abstraction while rebuilding its implementation for a new scale.
A process still executes a program in an address space. A file still
presents bytes. Those descriptions conceal increasingly sophisticated
policies about page replacement, caching, allocation, and scheduling.
Compatibility at the interface buys room for innovation underneath.

Virtual memory also changes the balance of costs. Copying an entire process
image at every creation may become expensive. Demand paging and
copy-on-write can reduce that expense, but they introduce page faults,
shared-state accounting, and new failure paths. A larger address space is
not free simply because the processor can express its addresses. It needs
an operating system that can manage the resulting work.

\section{The disk must keep up}
\index{Fast File System}\index{cylinder groups}

A faster processor and a larger memory expose a slow file system more
clearly. Workloads such as image processing and VLSI design moved large
amounts of data with relatively little computation per byte. A file system
using small blocks scattered across a disk could waste most of the device's
time seeking rather than transferring useful data.

The Fast File System described by McKusick, Joy, Leffler, and Fabry in 1984
addressed this mismatch. It used larger blocks for transfer efficiency,
fragments to avoid excessive waste for small files, and allocation policies
that improved locality. Cylinder groups placed related resources in
regions of the disk rather than leaving every allocation to an
undifferentiated free list \parencite{ffs}.

The paper reports performance in its own experimental and hardware setting.
Its headline improvements are not a timeless multiplier that can be
applied to every disk or application. The enduring result is the method:
measure the actual bottleneck, exploit the physical device's behavior,
and preserve a useful programming interface wherever possible.

FFS also included user-visible improvements such as longer filenames and
other facilities associated with the 4.2BSD environment. Not all of BSD's
contributions were invisible optimizations. The challenge was to introduce
new capabilities without forcing every existing program to understand
new disk geometry. The boundary between the file API and the allocation
algorithm made that possible.

\section{Networking becomes an ordinary programming task}
\index{TCP/IP}\index{sockets}\index{DARPA}

Networking research and government funding were important to Berkeley's
work. The 4.2BSD release in 1983 helped establish TCP/IP and the sockets
interface as widely available parts of a Unix programming environment.
The Internet was not invented by BSD, and TCP/IP did not originate there.
BSD's importance lay in integration, distribution, and the accessibility
of a working implementation to a community ready to use it.

A socket is represented by a descriptor, connecting network communication
to an existing style of I/O. Yet network setup needs operations with no
simple counterpart in opening a local file: choosing an address family,
binding a local endpoint, listening, connecting, and accepting a peer.
Sockets extended the file-descriptor model without pretending a network
was just an ordinary disk file.

That compromise was productive. Programs could use familiar reads and
writes once a connection existed, and event mechanisms could monitor
sockets alongside other descriptors. Specialized operations remained
available for specialized behavior. The resulting API was not perfectly
uniform, but it was usable and expressive enough to spread far beyond BSD
\parencite[ch. 2 and socket overview]{unp}.

Distribution mattered as much as invention. A networking stack available
with a widely studied operating system could be ported, debugged, taught,
and adapted. People learned sockets as an ordinary systems skill rather
than as a feature of one inaccessible research installation. Code,
manuals, and practice reinforced each other.

\section{A networked system has different weaknesses}

A time-sharing host already has concurrency and protection problems.
Connecting hosts adds partial failure. A remote machine may disappear
while the local one continues running. Delays may reflect congestion,
retransmission, a crashed service, or a slow application. A program can
remain correct with respect to its local API while making a bad assumption
about the state of a peer.

Network services also widen the audience for mistakes. A parser that once
handled input from a small group of local users may now receive arbitrary
bytes from strangers. Authentication, privilege separation, and resource
limits become critical. Convenience mechanisms designed for a trusted
laboratory network can become hazards on a hostile network.

The 1988 Internet worm demonstrated how connected environments could
magnify vulnerabilities and misplaced trust. It is misleading to infer
that networking destroyed a previously secure Unix. Multiuser protection
had limits long before the worm. Networking made those limits visible at
a larger scale and increased the cost of ignoring them.

\section{BSD as an integration tradition}

Berkeley's work is sometimes reduced to a branch on a family tree. That
image captures inheritance but underplays exchange. Code, techniques, and
interfaces moved between research Unix, commercial systems, Berkeley
releases, and later independent implementations. A clean tree can make a
history of borrowing look like a history of isolation.

The distributions also changed the meaning of a local improvement.
An editor fix could influence thousands of users; an API choice could
shape applications for decades. This increased the value of consistency,
release engineering, and compatibility. A research group that shipped a
system was taking responsibility for other people's work.

BSD's later legal separation from AT\&T code belongs in
\cref{ch:wars}. Technically, its earlier transformation was already
profound. Unix had become a comfortable environment for larger programs,
faster disks, and networked applications. Those capabilities made it an
attractive base for a new kind of computer: the personal, graphical,
network-connected workstation.
